Querying

MongoS 
- routes reads and writes to the relevant shards
- maintains a metadata cache of all the members
- clients and MongoS pick a MongoS with lowest latency from seedlist

during a query
lookup where data with the key is stored
send query to the replica set to run as normal
returns results back to client

if query does not contain shard key, send to all servers. results streamed back to client

How does the request path work?
Client -> MongoS -> Config server -> Shard server
Config server Replica Set (CSRS)

# Routing
MongoS extracts the shard key/prefix from the query
MongoS reads metadata from CSRS to determine where shard key is in the range.
If MongoS doesn't have this metadata cached, it does caches it.
Using the metadata MonoS routes the query to correct shard server.
Shard server runs the query and streams the results back to the client.
If the query cannot be targeted by shard, it broadcasts to all shards and streams results back to client
When metadata changes, cache is updated

Chunk migration and balancing
donors send data to other shards
and migrating/balancing can only occur on n/2 of all shards with this data. (prevent blocking other traffic)

